First run: Yu-Gi-Oh

2013 Dragon Rulers is nearly a textbook specimen.

At the 2013 South American WCQ, Konami reported:

* Dragon Ruler: 59
* Evilswarm: 49
* Spellbook: 36
* everything else much smaller. 

At the North American WCQ, Dragon Rulers occupied 13 of the top 20 seats in one top-table snapshot. 

Now watch the meta become self-aware.

Players weren’t merely asking “What makes my deck stronger?” Konami literally surveyed them on what they were using specifically to beat Dragon Rulers: Imperial Iron Wall, Vanity’s Emptiness, Necrovalley, Mind Drain and other targeted responses appeared. 

So:

Dragon Ruler strong

becomes:

Dragon Ruler common

which becomes:

beating Dragon Ruler unusually valuable

which changes:

optimal deck construction

That’s the moment the meta starts playing the game.

Then there is another player above all of them: the rule maker.

Konami changed the Forbidden & Limited List in August 2013, including forbidding the smaller “baby” Dragon Rulers and making broader changes to the environment. Konami explicitly described the following YCS as an “all-new era.” 

So the complete stack was:

cards
→ players
→ competitive meta
→ players exploiting expectations about meta
→ Konami altering the possibility space itself

The designer is playing a higher-order game.

