Your Blue-Eyes observation exposes another axis

This one is particularly useful.

Blue-Eyes can simultaneously possess different values in different metas:

Narrative meta: iconic, extraordinary, prestigious, scarce within the fiction.

Gameplay meta: determined by whatever the current card pool and competitive environment support.

Collector meta: individual printings can be highly scarce and valuable.

Physical-supply meta: “Blue-Eyes White Dragon” as a general card identity is far from inherently scarce because it has been printed repeatedly.

TCGplayer counted more than 50 English printings by 2024, ranging from inexpensive versions to exceptionally valuable specific versions. 

So “Blue-Eyes is rare” doesn’t have a single truth value.

It requires a coordinate:

Rare(Blue-Eyes | which meta?)

That’s Meta Displacement:

The same object can occupy radically different positions when moved between payoff systems.

A kid watching Yu-Gi-Oh might reasonably infer:

Blue-Eyes = supreme scarcity + prestige

and carry that mental valuation into collecting.

But the actual card economy says:

specific Blue-Eyes printing + condition + provenance + rarity → scarcity

not:

Blue-Eyes identity → scarcity

The television show therefore participates in the collector metagame even though it isn’t itself a card-market rule.

That is exactly the kind of cross-layer causality this instrument should catch.

