Pokémon gives us a different kind of meta evolution

Pokémon actively creates scheduled ecological disturbances.

For the 2026 Standard rotation, cards with the G regulation mark left the format while H, I, and J remained legal. Pokémon explicitly says rotation is intended to challenge players to develop new strategies and maintain a healthy competitive environment. 

That’s basically controlled forest fire ecology:

stable ecosystem → remove established resources → niches open → experimentation → new dominant populations

And right now you can see concentration again. Limitless’s current TEF–CRI aggregation has Dragapult variants comprising about 49% of its top-deck categorization, while other archetypes are much smaller. Pokémon’s own August 19 Worlds preview likewise frames Dragapult as the deck sitting at the top of the expected metagame. 

But here’s the interesting delta: the June 2026 NAIC winner was Lillie’s Clefairy, despite a Top 21 containing many Dragapult variants. 

That’s precisely why:

meta share ≠ deterministic superiority

A dominant meta creates ecological pressure that can sometimes make the correctly chosen minority strategy disproportionately valuable.

